From ec2421e2b30efcd4d8cbcfe325ac73ba73eaf5b2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ole Algoritme <olealgoritme@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 14:17:36 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] nak: if-conversion after register allocation (NAK_IF_CONVERT)

A divergent if/else costs NAK a BSSY, a BSYNC, the label NOP after it,
one or two branches and a BMOV.CLEAR around blocks that are often a few
instructions long, frequently just phi copies.  The reference compiler's
code for the heaviest Steel Nomad fragment shaders predicates about 3400
instructions; NAK's predicates none but branches.

opt_if_convert runs after jump threading and predicates a then-block of
at most 8 instructions, or a then- and else-block of at most 12
together, instead of branching around them.  After register allocation
this is exact: a predicated-off lane writes no register, which is all a
lane that skipped the block relied on.  Control, barrier and
warp-collective instructions, uniform instructions and blocks that write
the predicate stay branches.  A region left without branches loses its
BSSY/BSYNC pair, BMOV.CLEAR and label NOP, and blocks that now just fall
into each other are merged for the post-RA scheduler.  It cannot change
register counts.

NAK_NIR_FLATTEN=1 (default off) additionally lets
nir_opt_peephole_select flatten small ifs into selects (limit 8): fewer
instructions and static cycles, but the values of both sides are then
live at once, which costs occupancy on some CS2 shaders.

Offline, GB202: Steel Nomad fragment 143389 -> 142322 instructions,
compute 70416 -> 69781; CS2 compute -7.2% instructions, -10.5% static
cycles; no register count changes.
